\chapter{Literature Review}

\section{Near-field Array Processing}

Near-field array processing exploits the physical characteristics of wave propagation. As a signal radiates from a source, it initially exhibits a spherical wavefront. At sufficiently large distances, the wavefront can be approximated as planar across the receiving array. The transition between these propagation regimes is commonly described using the Fraunhofer distance, $r\approx\frac{2L^2}{\lambda}$, where $L$ is the maximum array aperture and $\lambda$ is the signal's wavelength. The region between the reactive near-field and the Fraunhofer far-field is referred to as the Fresnel, or radiating near-field, region where wavefront curvature remains significant\cite{array_freq_invariance}.

In far-field array processing, planar wavefronts produce approximately linear phase differences across sensors in the array. These phase differences depend primarily on the direction of arrival (DOA) of the incoming signal. As a result, far-field processing techniques typically estimate source direction, while range information is generally not directly observable. Localization of the source therefore often requires multiple spatially separated arrays or additional geometric constraints.

In contrast, near-field processing exploits spherical wavefront propagation, where phase differences between sensors depend on the absolute distance between each sensor and the source. The resulting steering vector becomes range-dependent, enabling simultaneous estimation of both source direction and range. Consequently, near-field processing allows a single array to perform source localization\cite{Adaptive_Beamforming}\cite{Sensor_Position}.

Near-field array processing has traditionally been applied to higher-frequency signals, where shorter wavelengths reduce the aperture required to observe wavefront curvature. Many established applications therefore exist in ultrasonic imaging, underwater acoustics, and acoustic holography\cite{array_freq_invariance}\cite{hiearchical_holography}. This frequency dependence has limited investigation of near-field localization techniques in lower-frequency acoustic environments, motivating further research in this area.

