\section{Active Noise Control Systems}

Active noise control (ANC) systems operate by generating an anti-noise signal that destructively interferes with unwanted acoustic disturbances. A reference microphone measures a noisy signal $x(n)$, which is processed by an adaptive filter to produce a cancelling signal $y(n)$. When reproduced through a loudspeaker, the cancelling signal is designed to have equal magnitude and opposite phase relative to the primary noise at a target location, resulting in destructive interference.

ANC systems are typically characterized by primary and secondary acoustic paths. The primary path describes the propagation of noise from the source to the error microphone, producing the primary disturbance signal $d(n)$. The secondary path represents the propagation of the cancelling signal from the loudspeaker through the acoustic environment to the error microphone. Because the secondary path modifies the amplitude and phase of the cancelling signal, it must be accounted for during adaptive filter design to ensure stable and effective noise cancellation\cite{FXLMS}.

In spatial ANC systems, the residual error signal is measured at specific spatial locations using one or more error microphones\cite{virt_mics}. These microphones provide feedback to the adaptive filter, allowing filter coefficients to be updated in order to minimize acoustic pressure at targeted listening regions. The spatial distribution of microphones and loudspeakers therefore plays a critical role in determining the effectiveness and coverage of the noise control region.

\section{Multi-Channel and Spatial Active Noise Control}

While single-channel active noise control (ANC) can reduce noise at a single point, real-world environments usually require suppression over a larger area. Multi-channel or spatial ANC systems tackle this by using multiple microphones and speakers to shape the sound field across a region or zone.

These systems coordinate multiple cancelling sources to generate interference patterns that cancel unwanted noise while preserving desired signals. Beamforming can be combined with adaptive filtering to improve spatial selectivity, allowing the system to focus cancellation on specific directions or locations.

Multi-channel ANC comes with added challenges. More sensors and speakers increase computational complexity, require modeling multiple secondary paths, and can introduce stability issues due to cross-channel interactions and changing acoustic conditions. Fully populated arrays are expensive and difficult to manage, which motivates more efficient approaches.

Incorporating spatial information, such as source localization or near-field array processing, offers a promising way to focus cancellation without needing full multi-channel systems. Even though this work focuses on a single cancelling source, the techniques for spatial localization and adaptive filtering are directly relevant to multi-channel ANC and represent a step toward more advanced spatial noise control\cite{Ear_field_ANC}\cite{Applied_ANC}.

\section{Adaptive Filtering Algorithms for ANC}

In the world of active noise control, several adaptive filtering algorithms are commonly used to estimate the transfer function between reference signals and error microphones. Three widely employed algorithms are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Least-Mean Squares (LMS)
    \item Recursive Least Squares (RLS)
    \item Fast Array Recursive Least Squares (FARLS)
\end{itemize}

The LMS algorithm begins with an initial estimate of the filter coefficients and updates them iteratively as new data is received. Updates are computed using a gradient descent approach to minimize the error signal energy. This makes LMS computationally simple and robust, but it typically converges more slowly than other algorithms, especially for longer filters or highly correlated inputs.

RLS algorithms exploit the structure of the input data to achieve faster convergence. By incorporating all past input data into a rank-1 update of the filter coefficients, RLS effectively "remembers" the history of the signal and can converge in approximately the length of the filter. However, this comes at the cost of significantly higher computational complexity compared to LMS, especially for multi-channel systems\cite{adp_fit_comp}.

FARLS is a variation of RLS designed to reduce computational demands while preserving fast convergence. It achieves this by transforming the standard RLS computations using Givens rotations along a hyperbolic curve to perform the rank-1 updates more efficiently. This approach reduces the number of operations required per iteration, making it well-suited for multi-sensor and spatial ANC applications where computational resources are limited.

FARLS is chosen for this work because it offers a combination of fast convergence and reduced computational complexity compared to standard RLS algorithms\cite{FARLS_EQ}. By efficiently updating filter coefficients using Givens rotations, FARLS can track time-varying signals more quickly while requiring fewer operations per iteration. This makes it particularly suitable for applications like spatial ANC, where multiple sensors and adaptive filters increase computational demands, and rapid adaptation is critical to effective noise cancellation.

\section{Snore Cancellation}

Snore cancellation has been studied for several years, with early approaches typically relying on one or two microphones to measure the snore signal. While these systems could effectively reduce noise at the microphone location, cancellation at the listener's ear was often limited due to poor spatial coverage\cite{Ear_field_ANC}\cite{Applied_ANC}\cite{RT_exp_ANC}\cite{subband_implementation}\cite{Online_secondary_path}\cite{Snore_detection_cancel}\cite{DSP_processor_snore}\cite{Joint_ANC}. Traditional implementations often used two large speakers mounted on a test headboard to generate the cancelling signal. Although functional, these setups were constrained by limited localization of the snore source, reducing the effectiveness of noise suppression in realistic listening positions.

Most existing snore cancellation systems use the Filtered-X Least Mean Squares algorithm, commonly abbreviated as FXLMS. FXLMS attempts to account for both the primary acoustic path from the snore source to the error microphone and the secondary path from the cancelling speaker to the microphone. This approach has been shown to work well in simulations and real-time experiments. However, the highly dynamic and non-stationary nature of snore signals motivates the exploration of alternative adaptive filtering methods.

In this work, the Fast Array Recursive Least Squares (FARLS) algorithm is proposed as an alternative. FARLS combines the fast convergence properties of the RLS algorithm with reduced computational complexity, making it well-suited for real-time applications\cite{FARLS_EQ}. While it introduces some numerical sensitivity, FARLS is expected to provide improved adaptation to time-varying snore signals and better performance in multi-sensor configurations compared to traditional FXLMS implementations.


